`#const` — Cross-Module Compile-Time Evaluation
================================================

The body of every non-generic `#const fn` declared in a module
round-trips through `.smeta` so any importing module can fold
`const X = lib::fn(7)` bindings at compile time. This file pins
that surface against the helper library in
`const_xmod_lib.lsysl`.

Scalar, aggregate, and float `#const fn` calls are all exercised
to confirm the driver re-analyzes each body in the importer's
context and routes the call through the embedded interpreter.

File-local prefix `Cxm` (Cross-module). Keep entries here in step
with `const_xmod_lib.lsysl` — adding a new `#const fn` in the lib
is only useful if a `Cxm` test binds against it.

    module sysl.tests.lang_features.effects.const_xmod_main

    import sysl.tests.lang_features.effects.const_xmod_lib.*


### Scalar `#const fn` import folds at the binding site

The binding `const CXM_DOUBLED = cxm_double(21)` triggers the
const-evaluation driver, which finds `cxm_double` in the
re-analyzed import set and folds the call to `42`.

    const CXM_DOUBLED: int = cxm_double(21)

    #test
    test_cxm_scalar_const_fn() -> unit
        assert(CXM_DOUBLED == 42, "CXM_DOUBLED should fold to 42")


### Chained imported `#const fn` calls fold

Two cross-module calls in one binding. Both `cxm_double` and
`cxm_inc` are imported; the driver resolves them as part of one
expression evaluation.

    const CXM_CHAINED: int = cxm_inc(cxm_double(20))

    #test
    test_cxm_chained_const_fn() -> unit
        assert(CXM_CHAINED == 41, "CXM_CHAINED should fold to 41")


### Imported `#const fn` body calls another imported `#const fn`

`cxm_double_then_inc` calls `cxm_inc` and `cxm_double` from its
own module. When re-analyzed in this module, those callees still
resolve correctly because they are visible through the same
wildcard import.

    const CXM_SELF_CALLED: int = cxm_double_then_inc(10)

    #test
    test_cxm_internal_const_fn_call() -> unit
        assert(CXM_SELF_CALLED == 21, "10 * 2 + 1 = 21")


### Aggregate-returning imported `#const fn`

`cxm_squares()` runs in the embedded interpreter and the resulting
`ArrVal` is reconstituted as a `TArrayLit` initializer in this
module. The binding materializes as a static `[4]int` block.

    const CXM_SQS: [4]int = cxm_squares()

    #test
    test_cxm_array_const_fn() -> unit
        assert(CXM_SQS[0] == 0, "CXM_SQS[0]")
        assert(CXM_SQS[1] == 1, "CXM_SQS[1]")
        assert(CXM_SQS[2] == 4, "CXM_SQS[2]")
        assert(CXM_SQS[3] == 9, "CXM_SQS[3]")


### Float `#const fn` import folds

`f64`-valued bindings round-trip through the bit-pattern carrier in
the `Const` SMETA line for scalar bindings, but `#const fn` results
ride through the body re-analysis path — this test pins that the
two stay consistent across modules.

    const CXM_HALF: f64 = cxm_to_half(7.0)

    #test
    test_cxm_float_const_fn() -> unit
        assert(CXM_HALF == 3.5, "7.0 * 0.5 = 3.5")
